Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Reflector Scrapes the Zenith
- Story & Context: During an illegal heatwave lock-in atop Antwerp's Cinema Rex, projectionist Dirk Vroom hauled a dented brass Newtonian telescope past the carbon-arc exhaust vents to track Comet Wilson across the chemical smog. When his grease-slicked thumb slipped on the declination gear, the heavy tube screeched violently against the corrugated zinc parapet just as his Pro-One sequencer locked into a manic 128 BPM bassline. Dirk grabbed an intercom mic and screamed over the unspooling acetate while patrons danced frantically beneath the open skylight: "We catch the white glare before the shutter bites!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: High-contrast graphic artwork of a heavy brass telescope aimed straight up into a murky, star-flecked night sky from an industrial rooftop, its polished metal barrel reflecting a blinding white film projector beam, vintage halftone printing, monochrome with splashes of deep chemical amber.

Track 2: "Hold the Eyepiece Steady, Karel"
- Story & Context: Huddled beneath a dripping canvas awning on the tar-paper roof of the Marollen archive depot, Marcella Vane confessed her mounting agoraphobia while cleaning soot from a chipped Barlow lens using the hem of her damp cardigan. She had spent forty straight hours scoring a non-existent silent thriller about astronomical spies, driving herself to exhaustion with screaming SH-101 leads that mimicked planetary radio noise. As Karel fumbled with the rusted tripod bolts in the cold autumn rain, Marcella looped her trembling baritone croon through an overdriven flanger: "Count the cold degrees until my blood turns silver."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Surrealist cinematic photograph from a rooftop perspective looking through a cracked telescope finder-scope at an oversized, distorted ringed planet, raindrops streaking across glass elements, illuminated by moody sodium vapor streetlights below.

Track 3: Film-Gate Parallax
- Story & Context: Decades after the venue was demolished, Belgian crate-diggers salvaged a water-damaged test pressing from the rubble of the Rex projection room where the duo performed their legendary final sunrise set. The track became folklore for its blistering monophonic synth lead that oscillated in sync with a malfunctioning projector gate, capturing the precise moment a solar eclipse projection melted into bubbling brown sludge. Squatters in the neighborhood still claim that playing the record at dawn summons the sharp, vinegar tang of burning nitrate stock along the chimney flues: "The reels are ash but the sky still marches."
